Yeah. There really is a version of this, but the surprising answer is not “rich people secretly have Instagram 2.” It is more like:

same civilization → different access layer.

The public sees the storefront. Once you cross certain wealth/status/professional thresholds, increasingly important things happen in vetted rooms, member directories, private introductions, professional messaging systems, and organizations whose value is who else has access.

A few concrete examples:

* Family offices are probably the closest thing to the phenomenon you mean. At sufficient wealth, you can effectively have a private institution wrapped around your family: investing, taxes, estate planning, security, bookkeeping, staff, philanthropy, travel, healthcare/concierge connections, and administration. J.P. Morgan’s 2026 family-office material explicitly discusses everything from bill pay and legal work to travel and healthcare concierge services. Their surveyed family offices represented an average participant net worth around $1.65 billion. 
* YPO is essentially a private network layer for qualifying chief executives. It reports more than 38,000 members, and its private networks span investing, deals, real estate, technology, sports/entertainment, fine dining, wine, parenting, etc. Qualification isn’t simply paying a subscription; there are executive authority, age, employee, revenue/assets and similar thresholds. 
* Hampton is a newer founder/CEO version. Its minimums include things such as $3M+ revenue, $3M+ raised, or a prior $10M+ exit, plus an interview. Members get small confidential peer groups and a private digital community specifically advertised for questions they wouldn’t post publicly. 
* Soho House is a softer cultural version: membership gives access to dozens of private club locations plus thousands of member events, and its app functions as a membership interface for benefits, events and access. It isn’t synonymous with “the rich,” but it demonstrates the pattern: the valuable social graph sits behind membership. 
* Raya is probably the most literal “there’s another app” example people know. It describes itself as a private community for dating, friendship and networking; applicants go through a review process before membership. But “everyone famous uses Raya” would be an exaggeration. 
* Bloomberg Terminal / Instant Bloomberg is a fantastic example because outsiders think of Bloomberg as financial news while users also get a professional communications network. Bloomberg says Terminal connects more than 350,000 financial professionals and decision-makers, with IB used for research, pricing, trade inquiries and market communication. It’s effectively a specialized information-and-relationship substrate sitting behind the consumer internet. 
* Private-market access is another largely invisible layer. U.S. securities law explicitly creates categories such as accredited investors and exempt offerings; some investment opportunities therefore do not need to be offered to the general public in the way public stocks are. Family-office and private-bank platforms also explicitly advertise access to private markets and direct opportunities. 

The pattern I’d put in our atlas is Access-Layer Stratification:

public interface
→ paid interface
→ credentialed interface
→ vetted community
→ trusted introductions
→ private bilateral relationships

And wealth is only one credential. Others are position, reputation, achievement, institution, family, profession, geography, or simply being vouched for by somebody already inside.

The especially interesting thing is that at the higher layers, the software matters less and the graph matters more.

Instagram might still be on their phone. The difference is that an ordinary person sees:

post → comments → DM

while someone embedded in a high-trust network can have:

private message → trusted introduction → private dinner → specialist → deal / opportunity / solution

So the genuinely “too poor to know about it” stuff often isn’t a secret product at all.

It’s a human API you haven’t been granted credentials to yet.